# Python / shell scripts are executed via their shebang (#!/usr/bin/env python3,
# #!/bin/sh, ...) during the CMake build and when running the Linux/Debian
# dedicated server. On Windows this repo is usually checked out with
# core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites them to CRLF. That CRLF survives onto the
# (shared) working tree, and when a script runs under Linux/WSL the kernel reads
# the shebang as "python3\r" and aborts:
#   env: 'python3\r': No such file or directory
#
# Force scripts to LF regardless of core.autocrlf so one checkout builds/runs on
# both Windows (MSYS handles LF fine) and Linux. These files are stored LF in the
# repo already, so this only corrects the working-tree checkout, not history.

*.py  text eol=lf
*.sh  text eol=lf

